{
 "description": "alarms.v2 §2.6, §2.16: one slot's samples, in the owner's order (core §4.2), and what a reader reads of each against the record it holds on the slot. Computed twice by two scripts of the text's drafter that share no code, kept out of the repository, and never blessed. A sample is {put: {run, alert_key, record}} or \"delete\" (a delete or a tombstone). `run` is the put's run (4 lowercase hexadecimal digits for a 16-bit run, 8 for a 32-bit run: two widths are two runs). `record` is the record's JSON reading (core §7.6): state, severity (null for the escape), at in milliseconds since the Unix epoch where present, rule, labels where present, argument, key where present; or \"undecodable\" for a put that does not read (records.json). `alert_key` is the key the reader has for the record: a record by words' own `key`, in 16 hexadecimal digits; the one it derives through the owner's table for a table's rule; or null for an unnamed alarm. Two records have one identity when each has an alert key and the two are equal, or when either has none and their `rule` and `labels` are equal, an absent labels equal only to an absent one. -> for each sample, {held, reads}. `held` is what becomes of a held record of another identity: `rebound` (it fired, under the put's run: its alarm reads resolved), `replaced` (it was resolved, under the put's run: nothing to read) or `dropped` (it was under another run: an earlier run's last word); null where nothing was held, or the same identity was. `reads` is the sample's own reading: raised, escalated, refreshed, confirmed, resolved, retired or unreadable. Against the same identity held firing, a firing put is escalated if the severity differs, else raised if `at` differs, else confirmed if the records are equal in every field, else refreshed; a resolved put is resolved. Against it held resolved, a firing put is raised; a resolved put is resolved if `at` differs, else confirmed if equal, else refreshed. An absent at is equal to an absent one and differs from any instant. Against nothing, and after `held`, a firing put is raised and a resolved one resolved. A delete is resolved over a firing record and retired over a resolved one or nothing, and the reader then holds nothing. After an unreadable put the reader holds nothing. The reader starts holding nothing. `reports`, where given, is what a notifier that reports changes (§2.16) reports after the sample, when every answer of §2.14 follows the slot's records alone (its owner present and listed, nothing lost, each firing record fresh): a list, empty for nothing to report, of {report, alert_key}: \"firing\" (that alarm's answer moved to yes, or its severity moved while yes) or \"resolved\" (it moved from yes to no: a resolved record, a delete or `rebound`). A record `replaced` or `dropped`, and an unreadable put, report nothing of the alarm held before.",
